# Mutation cases for §scxml-D-addAncestorStatesToEnter — a state entered only
# because the transition's target lies inside it takes NO default child.
#
# Run with `scripts/mutate sce-build/tests/mutations/ancestor_entry_is_not_default_entry_cpp.cases`.
#
# Appendix D asks two different questions with two functions.
# `addDescendantStatesToEnter` gives a compound state its default child and is
# called for the transition's TARGET; `addAncestorStatesToEnter` walks the
# states between the target and the LCCA and adds them WITHOUT defaults. In the
# C++ AOT backend that difference is spelled as `pathChild`: the entry walk
# hands every link BEFORE the target the next one, and `nullopt` from the
# target onwards, and `executeEntryActions` adds a default child only when
# `pathChild` is empty.
#
# ⚠ The target is the TEMPLATE, and that was measured rather than assumed.
# The first draft of this file aimed at `executeOnEntryChain` in
# `sce/include/static/StaticExecutionEngine.h`, which computes exactly the same
# `pathChild` — and both cases came back INCONCLUSIVE and SURVIVED. Applying
# the mutation by hand, rebuilding for 28s and running the oracles showed all
# 21 green: the generated transition code computes its OWN `pathChild` and
# never reaches the engine's copy for this shape of transition. A mutation that
# does not reach the code under test proves nothing about the tests that miss
# it.
#
# The template does reach the build, which was also measured rather than
# assumed: `add_custom_command`'s `DEPENDS` names only the SCXML and the
# codegen binary, so the templates look like non-dependencies — but a dry run
# after editing this file schedules `SCE: Generating ai_loop (cpp) from SCXML`,
# because the `Checking sce-codegen against the sources it was built from` step
# covers them.
#
# Why the clause needs a witness at all. It is asserted in three places and
# was witnessed in none: `integration_resources/ancestor_entry_is_not_default_entry/`
# pins it across seven channels, `examples/ai_loop/ai_loop.scxml` demonstrates
# it, and `AnsweringAQuestionDoesNotRePrimeTheSession` asserts it as the
# supervision bug it shipped as — and until this file nothing under
# `sce-build/tests/mutations/` broke the entry walk to find out whether any of
# them would notice.
#
# The defect it restores is not loud. Measured 2026-08-15 on both AOT engines
# with every W3C fixture green: an engine that gives every entered compound
# state its default leaves two of one state's children active at once, and in
# the worked example the extra child is `priming`, whose `<onentry>` sends the
# opening prompt. The symptom was a supervised session being re-sent its
# opening prompt every time a person answered a dialog. Nothing crashed.
#
# Both cases keep every local in use. A mutation that leaves `reachedTarget`
# written and never read does not compile under `-Werror`, and a case that
# cannot build has no witness at all — the round reports INCONCLUSIVE and the
# clause stays unmeasured, which is the state this file exists to end.

mutation_ctest --test-dir build -R '^IntegrationTests$'
mutation_targets tools/codegen/templates/process_transition.jinja2
mutation_oracles tests/integration/AncestorEntryIsNotDefaultEntryAotTest.cpp
mutation_oracles tests/integration/AiLoopAotTest.cpp

# ── The ancestor half is applied to the wrong half of the chain ────
#
# Dropping the `!` gives `pathChild` to the links AFTER the target and
# `nullopt` to the ancestors before it, which is Appendix D read backwards:
# every ancestor now takes its default initial child. One character, and it is
# the shape a reader writing this loop from the spec's prose can land on,
# because "the state we have reached" and "the states on the way to it" are one
# word apart.

mutation_case "ancestors take their default child and the target does not" <<'PY'
edit("tools/codegen/templates/process_transition.jinja2",
     "                        (!reachedTarget && chainIdx + 1 < entryChain.size())",
     "                        (reachedTarget && chainIdx + 1 < entryChain.size())")
PY

# ── The walk believes it is already past the target ────────────────
#
# The same defect reached from the other end: seeding the flag true makes every
# link read as "at or after the target", so the whole chain is entered with
# `nullopt` and every compound state on it takes its default. Worth pinning
# separately because it survives any rewrite of the conditional above.

mutation_case "the whole entry chain is walked as though it were the target" <<'PY'
edit("tools/codegen/templates/process_transition.jinja2",
     "                bool reachedTarget = false;",
     "                bool reachedTarget = true;")
PY
